<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
        <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
        <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>

        <!--
            ⚠ No generated address constants, and the properties that would ask for them are absent
            rather than set to true. `VixenAddressConstants` and friends are read by Vixen.Sdk's
            targets, and line 1 of this file is Microsoft.NET.Sdk — so setting them here changed
            nothing at all, while the comment that used to sit in their place described the
            generated file as something the build writes. An opt-in nothing imports is worse than an
            absent one, because a reader cannot tell.

            Turning it on is three edits and one bill. The edits: `Sdk="Vixen.Sdk/<version>"` on
            line 1; `VixenAddressConstants`, `VixenAddressNamespace` and `VixenProjectDirectory`
            pointing at ../VixenMmo1.Content; and `VixenContentBuildOnBuild=false` beside them,
            because the SDK's content build and copy-to-output belong to the client rather than to
            the assembly holding the rules. (`VixenAddressIds` adds a DefId per address and needs
            Vixen.Gameplay, which is a second opt-in.) The bill: the SDK carries the whole `vixen`
            CLI so that a consumer installs nothing, which is a large package on every machine that
            restores this project — see Vixen.Sdk's README before deciding.

            Until a game decides that, the addresses code names are constants somebody writes, and
            `vixen content build` over ../VixenMmo1.Content is a command rather than a build step.
        -->
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            ⚠ This project exists to stop one specific bug, and it is the classic one: the client's
            prediction and the server's simulation drifting into two implementations of one rule.
            docs/plan/27 § The three assemblies a game writes — the rules both ends run live here,
            once, and `MispredictionCount` in Vixen.Net.Prediction is the number that catches it when
            they do not.

            Referenced by the client and the realm. Not by the gate, and not by the orchestrator:
            neither of them simulates anything.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\VixenMmo1.Contracts\VixenMmo1.Contracts.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
